Changes made by Cloud/9:

  2024-03 to 2024-05
  - Recorded a new dataset of eight Indian Sign Language words (hi, my, name, is, what, your, glad, to) and the
    letters A, B and C, and trained a new model on it. The upstream model recognised only A, B and C.
  - function.py: the eight words replace A, B and C as the signs the model learns.
  - collectdata.py: keys 1-8 save word signs. app.py: shows the confidence as a percentage.
  - trainmodel.py: checked and padded keypoint shapes before training. Added info.py.

  2026-01 to 2026-10
  - function.py: paths relative to the project, load_trained_model() and load_dataset(), which turns frames where
    no hand was found into zeros and never unpickles a file.
  - trainmodel.py: fixed a crash on such frames, a fixed and stratified train/test split, held-out accuracy.
  - evaluate.py (new): accuracy, per-sign report and confusion matrix.
  - collectdata.py: rewritten around a key-to-folder table; Esc quits so that q still saves the letter Q.
  - data.py: explicit imports, skips missing images, saves zeros for frames without a hand.
  - dataset_tools/video_frame.py (new): frame extractor used to build the word dataset.
  - Tests, continuous integration and project documentation.

Each changed source file carries a notice pointing here.

Model weights (model.h5, model.json) were trained by Cloud/9 on the dataset below and are distributed under the
same license as the code.

The dataset (https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/paragtonpe/sign-language-recognition-dataset) was recorded by Cloud/9
and is published separately under CC BY-NC-SA 4.0 (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/).

Dependencies are installed from PyPI, not bundled. Each keeps its own license: TensorFlow, Keras, MediaPipe and OpenCV
(Apache License 2.0), NumPy and scikit-learn (BSD 3-Clause).
